{
  "version": 1,
  "title": "MVCC and write skew",
  "hook": "How does MVCC give readers a snapshot without blocking writers, and what is write skew?",
  "topic": "databases/txn/mvcc-snapshot-isolation",
  "tags": [
    "databases",
    "postgres",
    "mvcc",
    "isolation"
  ],
  "level": "advanced",
  "series": "databases-storage-engines",
  "series_index": 3,
  "scenes": [
    {
      "duration_ms": 11000,
      "caption": "In Postgres each row version has xmin and xmax; UPDATE adds a new version.",
      "narration": "In Postgres every tuple records xmin, the creating transaction, and xmax, the deleting one. An UPDATE writes a new tuple and stamps xmax on the old one.",
      "elements": [
        {
          "id": "h",
          "type": "title",
          "text": "Row versions",
          "x": 8,
          "y": 6,
          "w": 84,
          "at": 0,
          "enter": "fade"
        },
        {
          "id": "c",
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "text",
          "text": "SELECT xmin, xmax, *\nFROM accounts;",
          "x": 8,
          "y": 22,
          "w": 84,
          "at": 500,
          "enter": "slide-up"
        },
        {
          "id": "v1",
          "type": "box",
          "text": "v1 xmin=100 xmax=205",
          "x": 8,
          "y": 44,
          "w": 84,
          "h": 9,
          "color": "gray",
          "at": 3000,
          "enter": "pop"
        },
        {
          "id": "v2",
          "type": "box",
          "text": "v2 xmin=205 xmax=0",
          "x": 8,
          "y": 60,
          "w": 84,
          "h": 9,
          "color": "green",
          "at": 4500,
          "enter": "pop"
        },
        {
          "id": "a",
          "type": "arrow",
          "from": "v1",
          "to": "v2",
          "at": 4500,
          "enter": "draw",
          "label": "UPDATE"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "duration_ms": 12000,
      "caption": "A snapshot is (xmin, xmax, in-progress list); visibility is a comparison.",
      "narration": "A snapshot records the oldest active transaction, the next transaction ID, and in-progress IDs. A row is visible if its creator committed before the snapshot and its deleter did not.",
      "elements": [
        {
          "id": "h",
          "type": "title",
          "text": "Snapshot",
          "x": 8,
          "y": 6,
          "w": 84,
          "at": 0,
          "enter": "fade"
        },
        {
          "id": "c",
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "text",
          "text": "snapshot = {xmin, xmax,\n            xip[]}",
          "x": 8,
          "y": 22,
          "w": 84,
          "at": 500,
          "enter": "slide-up"
        },
        {
          "id": "l",
          "type": "list",
          "items": [
            "xmin must be committed, in snapshot",
            "xmax absent or not visible"
          ],
          "x": 8,
          "y": 42,
          "w": 84,
          "at": 3500,
          "enter": "slide-up"
        },
        {
          "id": "f",
          "type": "highlight",
          "target": "c",
          "at": 7500,
          "enter": "fade"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "duration_ms": 9000,
      "caption": "Readers read old versions; writers add new ones: no read-write blocking.",
      "narration": "Because readers see old versions and writers create new ones, neither blocks the other. VACUUM removes versions no snapshot can see.",
      "elements": [
        {
          "id": "h",
          "type": "title",
          "text": "No blocking",
          "x": 8,
          "y": 6,
          "w": 84,
          "at": 0,
          "enter": "fade"
        },
        {
          "id": "r",
          "type": "box",
          "text": "Reader: old version",
          "x": 8,
          "y": 22,
          "w": 38,
          "h": 12,
          "color": "blue",
          "at": 500,
          "enter": "pop"
        },
        {
          "id": "w",
          "type": "box",
          "text": "Writer: new version",
          "x": 54,
          "y": 22,
          "w": 38,
          "h": 12,
          "color": "green",
          "at": 500,
          "enter": "pop"
        },
        {
          "id": "n",
          "type": "text",
          "text": "VACUUM reclaims dead versions later.",
          "x": 8,
          "y": 46,
          "w": 84,
          "at": 4000,
          "enter": "fade"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "duration_ms": 14000,
      "caption": "Write skew: two txns read the same set, write different rows, no conflict.",
      "narration": "Under snapshot isolation, two transactions each check that two doctors are on call, then each takes a different doctor off. Neither touches the row the other changed, so both commit and nobody is on call.",
      "elements": [
        {
          "id": "h",
          "type": "title",
          "text": "Write skew",
          "x": 8,
          "y": 6,
          "w": 84,
          "at": 0,
          "enter": "fade"
        },
        {
          "id": "c",
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "text",
          "text": "T1: count(on_call)=2\n    UPDATE alice off\nT2: count(on_call)=2\n    UPDATE bob off",
          "x": 8,
          "y": 22,
          "w": 84,
          "at": 500,
          "enter": "slide-up"
        },
        {
          "id": "n",
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Both commit. Invariant \"at least one on call\" is broken.",
          "x": 8,
          "y": 54,
          "w": 84,
          "at": 6000,
          "enter": "fade",
          "color": "red"
        },
        {
          "id": "f",
          "type": "highlight",
          "target": "n",
          "at": 8500,
          "enter": "fade"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "duration_ms": 10000,
      "caption": "Fix it with SERIALIZABLE (SSI), or lock the rows you read.",
      "narration": "Postgres SERIALIZABLE uses serializable snapshot isolation to abort one of them. Alternatively use SELECT FOR UPDATE on the rows you read.",
      "elements": [
        {
          "id": "h",
          "type": "title",
          "text": "The fix",
          "x": 8,
          "y": 6,
          "w": 84,
          "at": 0,
          "enter": "fade"
        },
        {
          "id": "l",
          "type": "list",
          "items": [
            "ISOLATION LEVEL SERIALIZABLE",
            "Retry on 40001 serialization failure",
            "Or SELECT ... FOR UPDATE"
          ],
          "x": 8,
          "y": 22,
          "w": 84,
          "at": 500,
          "enter": "slide-up"
        },
        {
          "id": "f",
          "type": "highlight",
          "target": "l",
          "at": 6500,
          "enter": "fade"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "takeaway": "MVCC reads old row versions through a snapshot, but snapshot isolation allows write skew unless you serialize or lock what you read.",
  "sources": [
    "https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/mvcc-intro.html",
